{"abstract":"This study investigates the psychophysiological effects of loot box interactions in video games and their potential similarities to gambling-related behaviours. Using electrodermal activity (EDA) measurements, the research examines player arousal during loot box interactions and explores whether individuals with higher Internet Gaming Disorder (IGD) severity exhibit reduced sensitivity to these random reward mechanisms. The study employs a custom-designed game, ``A Minute Outside,'' to control experimental conditions and standardise loot box interactions. Participants' IGD severity is assessed using the Internet Gaming Disorder Scale – Short Form (IGDS9-SF), while arousal is measured through EDA, analysing skin conductance responses (SCRs), area under the curve (AUC), and skin conductance level (SCL). By leveraging psychophysiological methods commonly used in gambling research, this investigation aims to provide objective evidence on whether loot boxes function as gambling-like mechanisms at a physiological level. The study contributes to the ongoing debate surrounding gaming disorder and loot box harms, offering insights for game developers and policymakers on the potential risks associated with these monetisation strategies.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Tedeschi, Gianmarco","Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828182"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Electrodermal activity","Internet gaming disorder","Loot boxes","Psychophysiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"using psychophysiological insights to evaluate the impact of loot boxes on arousal","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.04906","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828182","ids":["itu-dcb7e191-405f-4067-8875-0bbd83af7cd9"],"keyword":["Electrodermal activity","Gambling-like mechanisms","Internet Gaming Disorder","Loot boxes","Psychophysiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-C0776280E6B7DB38B80B3C62FCC90E26"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.04906","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Game Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gianmarco","key":"tedeschipau","last":"Tedeschi","name":"Tedeschi, Gianmarco","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rune Kristian Lundedal","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"80784458-06f6-4eff-b0c8-df982ffa9835","name":"Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","orcid":"0000-0002-7209-9041","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.04906","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2025 IEEE Conference on Games","volume":"abs/2507.04906"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-dcb7e191-405f-4067-8875-0bbd83af7cd9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Using Psychophysiological Insights to Evaluate the Impact of Loot Boxes on Arousal","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828182","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background and Objectives As the gaming industry experiences exponential growth, concerns about gaming disorder (GD) also grow. It is crucial to understand the structural features of games that can interact with individual characteristics of gamers to promote GD. This research consolidates the views of an international body of panelists to create an assessment tool for gauging the addictive potential of distinct games. Methods Utilizing the iterative and structured Delphi method, an international panel of researchers, clinicians, and people with lived experience were recruited to offer a multifaceted viewpoint on the addictive risk associated with specific structural elements in games. Two rounds of surveys facilitated consensus. Results The panel initially included 40 members—ten from research, eight from clinical settings, and 22 with lived experiences. The second round included 27 panelists—seven from research, eight from clinical settings, and 11 with lived experiences. The study identified 25 structural features that contribute to potentially addictive gaming patterns. Discussion and Conclusions Consensus was found for 25 features, which were distilled into a 23-item evaluation tool. The Saini-Hodgins Addiction Risk Potential of Games Scale (SHARP-G) consists of five overarching categories: ‘Social,’ ‘Gambling-Like Features,’ ‘Personal Investment,’ ‘Accessibility,’ and ‘World Design.’ SHARP-G yields a total score indicating level of addiction risk. A case study applying the scale to three games of differing perceived risk levels demonstrated that that score corresponded to game risk as expected. While the SHARP-G scale requires further validation, it provides significant promise for evaluating gaming experiences and products.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Saini, Nirav","Adair, Cam","King, Daniel L.","Kuss, Daria J.","Gentile, Douglas A.","Kim, Hyoun S.","Edge, Jeremy","Billieux, Joël","Ng, John","Yun, Juliana P. S.","Henkel, Lisa","Faulcon, Linda","Nogueira, Michelle","Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","Husk, Shannon","Rumble, Shawn","Becker, Trey R.","Demetrovics, Zsolt","Hodgins, David C."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808911"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Canada","Gibraltar","Hungary","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","ELTE Eötvös Loránd University","Escapingthe.com","Flinders University","Game Quitters","Homewood Health Centre","Iowa State University","Nottingham Trent University","University of Calgary","University of Gibraltar","University of Lausanne"],"keyword":["Addiction risk assessment","Delphi method","Gaming disorder","Structural features"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"development of the saini hodgins addiction risk potential of games sharp g scale an international delphi study","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1556/2006.2024.00026","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-808911","identifier":[{"id":"85197988217","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5897a5ba-ded6-424f-8007-eb253f360e21"],"keyword":["Delphi method","addiction risk assessment","gaming disorder","structural features"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4C117D557D4936BCE9516AED9B66BE38"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://akjournals.com/view/journals/2006/13/2/article-p450.xml","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1556/2006.2024.00026","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109271078/2006-article-p450.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"university of calgary","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Calgary","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"game quitters","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Game Quitters","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"flinders university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Flinders University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"nottingham trent university","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Nottingham Trent University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"iowa state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Iowa State University","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"us","key":"escapingthe com","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Escapingthe.com","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"ch","key":"university of lausanne","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lausanne","used":1},{"affno":"8","country":"ca","key":"homewood health centre","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Homewood Health Centre","used":1},{"affno":"9","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Play, Culture and AI Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Game Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"10","country":"gi","key":"university of gibraltar","loc":["Gibraltar","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Gibraltar","used":1},{"affno":"11","country":"hu","key":"elte eötvös loránd university","loc":["Hungary","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"ELTE Eötvös Loránd University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nirav","key":"sainipau","last":"Saini","name":"Saini, Nirav","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Cam","key":"adairpau","last":"Adair","name":"Adair, Cam","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel L.","key":"kingpau","last":"King","name":"King, Daniel L.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Daria J.","key":"kusspau","last":"Kuss","name":"Kuss, Daria J.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Douglas A.","key":"gentilepau","last":"Gentile","name":"Gentile, Douglas A.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hyoun S.","key":"kimpau","last":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyoun S.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeremy","key":"edgepau","last":"Edge","name":"Edge, Jeremy","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"Joël","key":"billieuxpau","last":"Billieux","name":"Billieux, Joël","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"ngpau","last":"Ng","name":"Ng, John","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Juliana P. S.","key":"yunpau","last":"Yun","name":"Yun, Juliana P. S.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lisa","key":"henkelpau","last":"Henkel","name":"Henkel, Lisa","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Linda","key":"faulconpau","last":"Faulcon","name":"Faulcon, Linda","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"8","corresponding":null,"first":"Michelle","key":"nogueirapau","last":"Nogueira","name":"Nogueira, Michelle","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9","corresponding":null,"first":"Rune K. L.","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"80784458-06f6-4eff-b0c8-df982ffa9835","name":"Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","name_org":"Nielsen, Rune K. L.","orcid":"0000-0002-7209-9041","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Shannon","key":"huskpau","last":"Husk","name":"Husk, Shannon","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Shawn","key":"rumblepau","last":"Rumble","name":"Rumble, Shawn","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Trey R.","key":"beckerpau","last":"Becker","name":"Becker, Trey R.","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 10 11","corresponding":null,"first":"Zsolt","key":"demetrovicspau","last":"Demetrovics","name":"Demetrovics, Zsolt","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"David C.","key":"hodginspau","last":"Hodgins","name":"Hodgins, David C.","rank":19,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1556/2006.2024.00026","issn":["20625871","20635303"],"issn_org":["20625871"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Behavioral Addictions","pages":"450-462","publisher":"Akadémiai Kiadó","status":"p","title":"Journal of Behavioral Addictions","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85197988217"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5897a5ba-ded6-424f-8007-eb253f360e21"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Development of the Saini-Hodgins Addiction Risk Potential of Games (SHARP-G) Scale : An International Delphi study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808911","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Loot boxes can be bought with real-world money to obtain random content inside video games (Drummond and Sauer 2018). Loot boxes are viewed by many as gambling-like and are prevalently implemented globally (Xiao 2023; Xiao, Henderson, and Newall 2023; 2022; Zendle et al. 2020; 2022), including in the People’s Republic of China (PRC). Previous Western and international studies have consistently found loot box spending to be positively correlated with problem gambling (Zendle and Cairns 2018; Spicer et al. 2021; Garea et al. 2021). Previous Western studies presented mixed results as to the correlations between loot box purchasing and gambling-related risk factors, mental wellbeing, and psychological distress (Etchells, Morgan, and Quintana 2022; cf. Drummond, Hall, and Sauer 2022). A large-scale survey of adult video game players from Mainland China (N = 2,601) was conducted through Tencent Survey to assess the relationships between loot box spending and potential gambling-related risk factors and mental health. The positive correlations between loot box spending and problem gambling, and between loot box spending and problem videogaming, were successfully replicated. However, other potential risk factors (i.e., impulsivity/impulsiveness; binary past-year gambling participation status; and sensation-seeking tendencies) either did not positively correlate with loot box spending or only did so weakly. Contrary to expectations (see Garrett et al. 2023), high impulsivity was negatively associated with loot box engagement. The Risky Loot Box Index (RLI) (see Brooks and Clark 2019) most strongly positively correlated with, and was the best predictor in multiple linear regression models for, loot box spending. The RLI may be effective at measuring loot box harms cross-culturally (see Forsström et al. 2022). A surprising weak positive correlation was found between loot box engagement and PRC players’ mental wellbeing, and high psychological distress unexpectedly negatively predicted loot box purchasing. No evidence was found that loot box engagement correlated negatively with mental wellbeing or positively with psychological distress amongst PRC video game players. In fact, there was weak support for the opposite. The practical harms of engaging with loot boxes (if any) need further elucidation. Cross-cultural research can contribute to a better understanding of loot box harms.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Xiao, Leon Y.","Fraser, Tullia C.","Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","Newall, Philip W.S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718826"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bristol","University of Glasgow"],"keyword":["Computer games","Loot boxes","Mainland China","Problem gambling","videogaming"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"loot boxes gambling related risk factors and mental health in mainland china a large scale survey","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/qgbe6","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718826","ids":["itu-091bf607-b8a6-41f2-af25-af985888d17d"],"keyword":["Mainland China","Problem gambling","computer games","loot boxes","videogaming"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-7DA6F0BEC51E1BA3F8C296DD086CE453"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101903945/PRC_LB_Survey_Redux_CDiGRA_2023.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.31219/osf.io/qgbe6","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of glasgow","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Glasgow","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon Y.","key":"xiaopau","last":"Xiao","locid":"0a2a2206-180b-4443-bdb7-92deba889221","name":"Xiao, Leon Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-0709-0777","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Tullia C.","key":"fraserpau","last":"Fraser","name":"Fraser, Tullia C.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rune Kristian Lundedal","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"80784458-06f6-4eff-b0c8-df982ffa9835","name":"Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","orcid":"0000-0002-7209-9041","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Philip W.S.","key":"newallpau","last":"Newall","name":"Newall, Philip W.S.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.31219/osf.io/qgbe6","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 10th Annual Chinese Digital Games Research Association (CDiGRA) Conference 2023 (Chinese DiGRA 2023)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-091bf607-b8a6-41f2-af25-af985888d17d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Loot boxes, gambling-related risk factors, and mental health in Mainland China: A large-scale survey","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718826","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
