{"abstract":"Internationally, several policies have been designed to prevent pathological or “problematic” gaming issues in youth, commonly referred to simply as “game addiction.” Particularly following the release of the World Health Organization’s “gaming disorder” diagnosis, policymakers may be inclined to enact further policies on this matter. With new data reflecting lack of success for South Korea’s shutdown policy, the efficacy of current policy efforts remains in doubt. Given continued controversies regarding whether pathological gaming or gaming disorder is best conceptualized as a unique disorder rather than symptomatic of other, underlying mental illnesses, little data has emerged to encourage policy interventions. By contrast, policy interventions at this juncture may risk doing considerable harm and wag the dog in the sense of reifying a pathological gaming disorder construct that remains problematic and under contentious debate in the field. We advise caution, ethnographic and qualitative research approaches, open science, etiological comprehension, and more time to fully understand whether pathological gaming is the best target for policy interventions and informing clinicians. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)","author_count":4,"contributors":["Ferguson, Christopher J","Bean, Anthony","Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","Smyth, Mark P"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384052"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Ireland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Framingham State University","Psychological Society of Ireland","Stetson University"],"keyword":["Ethnographic research","Gaming Disorder Diagnosis","Mental health","Pathological gaming","Policy Interventions"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"policy on unreliable game addiction diagnoses puts the cart before the horse","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84359286/Accepted_author_manuscript_Ferguson_Bean_Nielsen_Smyth_2019_Policy_on_Unreliable_Problematic_Gaming_Diagnoses_Puts_the_Horse_Before_the_Cart._Psychology_of_Popular_Media_Culture.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384052","identifier":[{"id":"85067105226","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-be093edc-d28d-425e-ad4d-aabb97d2de4a"],"keyword":["Ethnographic Research","Gaming Disorder Diagnosis","Mental Health","Pathological Gaming","Policy Interventions"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-6BC0609FBC6FC8CC273124EFF9EC8208"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84359286/Accepted_author_manuscript_Ferguson_Bean_Nielsen_Smyth_2019_Policy_on_Unreliable_Problematic_Gaming_Diagnoses_Puts_the_Horse_Before_the_Cart._Psychology_of_Popular_Media_Culture.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"stetson university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Stetson University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"framingham state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Framingham State University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"ie","key":"psychological society of ireland","loc":["Ireland","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Psychological Society of Ireland","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher J","key":"fergusonpau","last":"Ferguson","name":"Ferguson, Christopher J","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anthony","key":"beanpau","last":"Bean","name":"Bean, Anthony","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Rune Kristian Lundedal","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"80784458-06f6-4eff-b0c8-df982ffa9835","name":"Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","orcid":"0000-0002-7209-9041","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark P","key":"smythpau","last":"Smyth","name":"Smyth, Mark P","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1037/ppm0000249","issn":["21604134","21604142"],"issn_org":["21604134"],"journal":"Psychology of Popular Media Culture","publisher":"American Psychological Association","status":"p","title":"Psychology of Popular Media Culture"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85109944621"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-be093edc-d28d-425e-ad4d-aabb97d2de4a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Policy on unreliable game addiction diagnoses puts the cart before the horse","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384052","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
